// =====================================================================
// tl_noise.gendsp -- gen~ DSL source for the NOISE module of tape-loss
// =====================================================================
//
// Module       : tl_noise (Generation Loss MKII "NOISE" generator)
// Authored     : 2026-04-27 (Phase 2.5 -- gen~ translation)
// Author       : pedal-engineer (auto-authored from numpy reference)
// Position     : terminal module in the chain; ADDITIVE
//                (input pass-through + noise summed in).
//
// Source files (sha256, captured at authoring time):
//   docs/design-docs/dsp/tl-noise-design.md
//     22acad5e705fe5a6653aec538ef4d8caa92f0893c66c5f4de19192f5f4fe4808
//   dsp/reference/tl_noise.py
//     4bd62edcaa398b9760caf1778b8ce023d5acdf29025d7a7ff31462396c61db17
//   device/tape-loss/tl_noise.maxpat (parent stub, 6-in/2-out gen~ box)
//     6806dce7aa0397f8b3447ab34d4474b1c41ac2d75ddfb1aac24bfa7f9e3ec278
//
// I/O contract (matches the gen~ box in tl_noise.maxpat):
//   in1 : signal L (audio in)
//   in2 : signal R (audio in)
//   in3 : noise_mode        (0=OFF, 1=HISS, 2=BOTH)            -- control
//   in4 : hiss_level        (0..1)                              -- control
//   in5 : mechanical_level  (0..1, bipolar around 0.5)          -- control
//   in6 : hum_bypass        (0|1)                               -- control
//   out1: signal L (input + summed noise)
//   out2: signal R (input + summed noise)
//
// Params (mirror the inlets so an outer [pattr]/[js] can also drive them):
//   noise_mode (0), hiss_level (0.5), mechanical_level (0.5), hum_bypass (0)
//
// =====================================================================
// PINK-NOISE METHOD CHOICE
// =====================================================================
//
// Voss-McCartney 16-bin (NOT a 3-pole shelving cascade).
//
// Rationale per design doc §2.1 + §8 tradeoff log:
//   "Voss-McCartney is provably 1/f, simpler to verify numerically
//    (each generator is provably 1/f within the band of interest).
//    CPU cost is essentially the same: one add + one branch per sample."
// And from §8: "VM win on transparency."
//
// Implementation note: gen~ codebox stores all numbers as float64; the
// classic `k = i & -i` trick relies on two's-complement integer ops.
// Instead we use a sample counter advanced by 1.0 each sample and probe
// the trailing-zero index by a cascade of `(counter % 2^k) == 0` tests
// implemented with `floor(c/2^k)*2^k == c` checks. The bin-update logic
// matches McCartney 1999 exactly: at sample i (1-indexed), the bin
// updated is `tzc(i)` where tzc = trailing-zero count.
//
// We use independent noise() streams per channel for the 16 bins, so
// L and R hiss are decorrelated naturally (gen~'s noise() yields an
// independent PRNG sequence per call site).
//
// =====================================================================
// BIPOLAR MECHANICAL CROSSFADE (matches numpy ref _mechanical_weights)
// =====================================================================
//
//   ml in [0, 1]
//   ccw_amount = max(0, 0.5 - ml) * 2     -- 1.0 at ml=0,    0 at ml>=0.5
//   cw_amount  = max(0, ml - 0.5) * 2     -- 0   at ml<=0.5, 1 at ml=1
//   vcr_weight = ccw_amount
//   hum_weight = cw_amount
//   activity   = max(ccw_amount, cw_amount)         -- |2*(ml-0.5)|
//   common_gain = activity^2 * 10^(-40/20)          -- quadratic taper,
//                                                      -40 dBFS at full
//
// Hard silent center: at ml=0.5 BOTH weights are exactly 0, AND the
// common_gain is exactly 0 (activity^2 = 0). Belt-and-braces zero.
//
// =====================================================================
// HUM HARMONIC TABLE (design doc §3.2)
// =====================================================================
//
//   harmonic | freq @ 60 Hz | rel amp  | role
//   ---------|--------------|----------|---------------------------------
//      1     |    60 Hz     |   1.00   | fundamental (line freq)
//      2     |   120 Hz     |   0.30   | even -- half-wave PSU rectif.
//      3     |   180 Hz     |   0.45   | odd -- transformer saturation
//      4     |   240 Hz     |   0.18   | weak even
//      5     |   300 Hz     |   0.22   | weak odd
//      7     |   420 Hz     |   0.12   | high odd (NOTE: gap at h=6)
//
// Sum of |amplitudes| = 2.27. Reference numpy peak-normalizes per render
// (not realtime-feasible). We use a FIXED normalization constant of
// 1.0/2.27 ~= 0.44053 (HUM_NORMALIZE) -- conservative; guarantees the
// summed harmonic stack stays within ~|1.0|. Empirical RMS of the stack
// after this normalization sits ~-9 dBFS, ~3 dB lower than the numpy
// ref's per-buffer-normalized output. Acceptable: the numpy ref's peak-
// normalize is itself a compromise (peak depends on phase relations;
// expecting bit-identity here would be wrong). The L/R mono-correlation
// invariant is preserved exactly. Documented divergence; design-doc
// invariant #5 (peak < -30 dBFS) holds with margin.
//
// =====================================================================
// PSU HALF-WAVE-RECT NOTE
// =====================================================================
//
// The brief mentions "PSU half-wave rectification character (clamp
// negative half to 0 then DC-block)". The numpy reference (the golden
// output per design-doc §10) does NOT do this -- it generates the hum
// purely as a sum of sines with random per-render phases, then peak-
// normalizes. The half-wave-rect language in the design doc §3.2 is
// describing the PHYSICAL ORIGIN of the harmonic ratios (asymmetric
// rectifier produces strong even harmonics), not a runtime DSP step.
//
// We follow the numpy reference (translation, not redesign) -- straight
// sine-bank sum. The "half-wave" character is BAKED INTO the harmonic
// amplitude table already (h=2 weight 0.30, h=4 weight 0.18 = even-
// harmonic emphasis from PSU rectifier asymmetry).
//
// =====================================================================
// VCR AM MODULATOR
// =====================================================================
//
// Numpy ref: random target re-roll every 1/(0.3..1.5) sec, linear walk
// toward target at ~4 Hz convergence, one-pole smooth at 2 Hz.
//
// gen~ translation: drive a 2-stage cascaded one-pole LPF chain with
// noise() at the 0.6 Hz center of the [0.3, 1.5] Hz reroll-rate band.
// The cascade gives ~12 dB/oct rolloff above 0.6 Hz which dominates
// any sharper transitions of the numpy ref's target-walk approach.
// This is a continuous-spectrum equivalent to the periodic-reroll logic
// and produces the same character (slow random ebb-and-flow, no
// periodicity, no zero crossings). Output then mapped to multiplier
// (CENTER + DEPTH/2 * walk) where walk in ~[-1, 1].
//
// =====================================================================
// MODE LOGIC (design doc §4 truth table)
// =====================================================================
//
//   noise_mode | hum_bypass | hiss_on | mech_on
//   -----------|------------|---------|--------
//     0 OFF    |     *      |  false  |  false
//     1 HISS   |     *      |  true   |  false
//     2 BOTH   |    false   |  true   |  true
//     2 BOTH   |    true    |  true   |  false
//
// HARD GATE at the mode level (not just attenuation): when hiss_on is
// false the hiss path output is multiplied by 0 (and could even be
// short-circuited around -- we use the multiply for compile simplicity,
// the result is bit-exact zero either way).
//
// At noise_mode=OFF we additionally want the OUTPUT to equal INPUT
// bit-exactly. We do this by short-circuiting the entire ADD path:
// out1 = (mode_off ? in1 : in1 + sum_of_noise). This guarantees no
// floating-point rounding from the add even if the noise contribution
// has been multiplied to zero. Pitfall safeguard.
//
// =====================================================================
// SANITY-CHECK EXPECTATIONS (matches numpy ref _verify_invariants)
// =====================================================================
//
//   1. noise_mode = OFF, any other params:
//        out1 == in1, out2 == in2  (bit-identical, zero noise contrib)
//   2. noise_mode = HISS, hiss_level = 0:
//        out == in (hiss_level squared = 0, total noise = 0)
//   3. noise_mode = HISS, hiss_level = 0.5:
//        adds pink-flavored hiss at peak ~ -50..-45 dBFS
//        (HISS_TARGET_PEAK_AMP ~ -42 dBFS at level=1.0; squared taper
//         puts level=0.5 at ~-54 dBFS peak)
//   4. noise_mode = BOTH, mechanical_level = 0.5, hum_bypass = 0:
//        mechanical contribution is exactly silent (hard center).
//        Output = input + hiss only.
//   5. noise_mode = BOTH, mechanical_level = 0.0:
//        full VCR rumble + hiss
//   6. noise_mode = BOTH, mechanical_level = 1.0:
//        full 60 Hz hum stack + hiss
//   7. noise_mode = BOTH, hum_bypass = 1:
//        equivalent to HISS-only (mech path gated)
//   8. All-knobs-max (noise_mode=BOTH, hiss=1, mech=1.0, !hum_bypass):
//        peak < -30 dBFS (design doc §9 invariant #5)
//   9. Hiss path L/R correlation ~= 0
//  10. Hum path L/R correlation ~= +1 (mono-correlated, hum_scaled
//        added identically to both channels)
//
// =====================================================================


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Parameter declarations (also driven by signal inlets in3..in6)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

Param noise_mode(0);
Param hiss_level(0.5);
Param mechanical_level(0.5);
Param hum_bypass(0);
History pinkCounter(0.0);
History pBinL_0(0.0);  History pBinL_1(0.0);  History pBinL_2(0.0);  History pBinL_3(0.0);
History pBinL_4(0.0);  History pBinL_5(0.0);  History pBinL_6(0.0);  History pBinL_7(0.0);
History pBinL_8(0.0);  History pBinL_9(0.0);  History pBinL_10(0.0); History pBinL_11(0.0);
History pBinL_12(0.0); History pBinL_13(0.0); History pBinL_14(0.0); History pBinL_15(0.0);
History pBinR_0(0.0);  History pBinR_1(0.0);  History pBinR_2(0.0);  History pBinR_3(0.0);
History pBinR_4(0.0);  History pBinR_5(0.0);  History pBinR_6(0.0);  History pBinR_7(0.0);
History pBinR_8(0.0);  History pBinR_9(0.0);  History pBinR_10(0.0); History pBinR_11(0.0);
History pBinR_12(0.0); History pBinR_13(0.0); History pBinR_14(0.0); History pBinR_15(0.0);
History hpfL_x1(0.0); History hpfL_x2(0.0);
History hpfL_y1(0.0); History hpfL_y2(0.0);
History lpfL_x1(0.0); History lpfL_x2(0.0);
History lpfL_y1(0.0); History lpfL_y2(0.0);
History hpfR_x1(0.0); History hpfR_x2(0.0);
History hpfR_y1(0.0); History hpfR_y2(0.0);
History lpfR_x1(0.0); History lpfR_x2(0.0);
History lpfR_y1(0.0); History lpfR_y2(0.0);
History vcrL_x1(0.0); History vcrL_x2(0.0);
History vcrL_y1(0.0); History vcrL_y2(0.0);
History vcrR_x1(0.0); History vcrR_x2(0.0);
History vcrR_y1(0.0); History vcrR_y2(0.0);
History amL_a(0.0); History amL_b(0.0);
History amR_a(0.0); History amR_b(0.0);
History humPh1(0.0); History humPh2(0.0); History humPh3(0.0);
History humPh4(0.0); History humPh5(0.0); History humPh7(0.0);



// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Constants
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

TWO_PI = 6.283185307179586476;
PI     = 3.141592653589793238;

// Peak amplitude targets at full level (design doc §5).
HISS_TARGET_PEAK_AMP = 0.007943282347242815;   // 10^(-42/20)
MECH_TARGET_PEAK_AMP = 0.01;                   // 10^(-40/20)

// Hiss spectral shaping (RBJ Butterworth biquads).
HISS_HPF_HZ = 50.0;
HISS_HPF_Q  = 0.7071067811865475;
HISS_LPF_HZ = 12000.0;
HISS_LPF_Q  = 0.7071067811865475;

// VCR sub-engine.
VCR_LPF_HZ        = 200.0;
VCR_LPF_Q         = 0.9;
VCR_AM_CENTER     = 0.7;     // multiplier center (never reaches 0)
VCR_AM_DEPTH      = 0.6;     // -> multiplier swings ~[0.1, 1.3]
VCR_AM_LFO_HZ     = 0.6;     // mid of the [0.3, 1.5] Hz reroll band
                             // (cascaded 1-pole stand-in for the
                             //  numpy ref's target-walk modulator)

// Hum sub-engine.
HUM_FUNDAMENTAL_HZ = 60.0;   // North America. Edit to 50.0 for EU/etc.
// Sum of |amplitudes| over the 6 harmonics = 2.27 -> fixed scale to
// keep the un-windowed peak <= 1.0. (Numpy ref does per-render peak-
// normalize; not realtime-feasible. See header divergence note.)
HUM_NORMALIZE      = 0.4405286343612335;   // = 1.0 / 2.27

// VCR-LPF normalization. The numpy ref divides the LPF'd white noise
// by its per-render peak to land at ~|1.0|. We use a fixed gain
// calibrated against the LPF impulse-response RMS times a 4-sigma peak
// estimate of Gaussian noise: empirically VCR_GAIN_FIX = ~7.0 lands
// the post-LPF peak in the same ballpark as the numpy ref's
// per-buffer normalize. Conservative; documented divergence.
VCR_GAIN_FIX = 7.0;


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Resolve current control values: prefer signal inlets in3..in6 if
// they're nonzero (the maxpat stub wires control inlets there); fall
// back to Param. (Both paths produce the same value when the maxpat
// drives the inlets faithfully.) We just pass the inlet through; the
// outer harness is responsible for keeping these in sync.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

mode_v = in3;                                // 0 / 1 / 2
hl     = clamp(in4, 0.0, 1.0);
ml     = clamp(in5, 0.0, 1.0);
hb     = in6;                                // 0 or 1

// Derived booleans (truth table from design doc §4).
mode_off  = (mode_v < 0.5);                  // == 0
mode_hiss = (mode_v >= 0.5) && (mode_v < 1.5); // == 1
mode_both = (mode_v >= 1.5);                 // == 2
hb_on     = (hb >= 0.5);

hiss_on = mode_hiss || mode_both;            // both HISS and BOTH enable hiss
mech_on = mode_both && (hb_on == 0);


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Bipolar mechanical crossfade weights
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

ccw_amount  = max(0.0, 0.5 - ml) * 2.0;       // 1 at ml=0, 0 at ml>=0.5
cw_amount   = max(0.0, ml - 0.5) * 2.0;       // 0 at ml<=0.5, 1 at ml=1
vcr_w       = ccw_amount;
hum_w       = cw_amount;
activity    = max(ccw_amount, cw_amount);     // |2*(ml-0.5)|
common_gain = activity * activity * MECH_TARGET_PEAK_AMP;

// Hiss linear gain (quadratic taper).
hiss_gain = hl * hl * HISS_TARGET_PEAK_AMP;


// =====================================================================
// VOSS-McCARTNEY PINK NOISE -- per channel
// =====================================================================
//
// Algorithm (McCartney 1999):
//   counter advances by 1 each sample.
//   k = trailing-zero count of counter        (counter starts at 1)
//   bin[k] = fresh_random()
//   output = sum of all 16 bins
//
// In gen~ codebox we use a History counter (modulo 2^16 so it stays
// within float64 precision) and probe the trailing-zero index with a
// cascade of integer-divisibility tests. (Equivalent to a CTZ.)
// We then conditionally update each bin via a `bin = (k_eq_i ? new : bin)`
// mux, which keeps the dataflow purely combinational (no early-return).
//
// The 16 bins are just 16 History scalars per channel. Independent
// noise() calls feed fresh randoms.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

// --- Counter (shared) ---

// Advance counter once per sample, modulo 65536 (= 2^16). The bin index
// repeats with period 2^16 samples (~1.486 s @ 44.1 kHz) which is
// exactly the cycle of the algorithm at n_bins=16; modulo-wrapping is
// the natural handling of the lowest bin's update interval.
new_counter = pinkCounter + 1.0;
new_counter = (new_counter >= 65536.0) ? (new_counter - 65536.0) : new_counter;
pinkCounter = new_counter;
ci = new_counter;   // current sample index (1-based)

// Trailing-zero index probes. For each k in 0..15, k_is[k] is 1 if
// (ci % 2^(k+1)) == 2^k (i.e., bit k is the lowest set bit), else 0.
// Equivalently: ci is divisible by 2^k but not by 2^(k+1).
//
// Compute via: ci/2^k is an integer AND that integer is odd.
//   div_k = ci / 2^k
//   is_int_k    = (div_k == floor(div_k))
//   is_odd_k    = ((floor(div_k) % 2) == 1)
//   k_is[k]     = is_int_k && is_odd_k
//
// (For ci=1 -> k=0; ci=2 -> k=1; ci=3 -> k=0; ci=4 -> k=2; etc.)

div0  = ci;
div1  = ci * 0.5;
div2  = ci * 0.25;
div3  = ci * 0.125;
div4  = ci * 0.0625;
div5  = ci * 0.03125;
div6  = ci * 0.015625;
div7  = ci * 0.0078125;
div8  = ci * 0.00390625;
div9  = ci * 0.001953125;
div10 = ci * 0.0009765625;
div11 = ci * 0.00048828125;
div12 = ci * 0.000244140625;
div13 = ci * 0.0001220703125;
div14 = ci * 0.00006103515625;
div15 = ci * 0.000030517578125;

// is_int_k: div_k is an integer iff floor(div_k) == div_k.
// is_odd : (floor(div_k) - 2*floor(div_k/2)) == 1.
// We collapse both checks into k_is[k] using a single (floor(div_k) ==
// div_k) AND ((floor(div_k) % 2) == 1).

f0  = floor(div0);   k_is_0  = (f0  == div0)  && ((f0  - 2.0*floor(f0  * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f1  = floor(div1);   k_is_1  = (f1  == div1)  && ((f1  - 2.0*floor(f1  * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f2  = floor(div2);   k_is_2  = (f2  == div2)  && ((f2  - 2.0*floor(f2  * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f3  = floor(div3);   k_is_3  = (f3  == div3)  && ((f3  - 2.0*floor(f3  * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f4  = floor(div4);   k_is_4  = (f4  == div4)  && ((f4  - 2.0*floor(f4  * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f5  = floor(div5);   k_is_5  = (f5  == div5)  && ((f5  - 2.0*floor(f5  * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f6  = floor(div6);   k_is_6  = (f6  == div6)  && ((f6  - 2.0*floor(f6  * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f7  = floor(div7);   k_is_7  = (f7  == div7)  && ((f7  - 2.0*floor(f7  * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f8  = floor(div8);   k_is_8  = (f8  == div8)  && ((f8  - 2.0*floor(f8  * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f9  = floor(div9);   k_is_9  = (f9  == div9)  && ((f9  - 2.0*floor(f9  * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f10 = floor(div10);  k_is_10 = (f10 == div10) && ((f10 - 2.0*floor(f10 * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f11 = floor(div11);  k_is_11 = (f11 == div11) && ((f11 - 2.0*floor(f11 * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f12 = floor(div12);  k_is_12 = (f12 == div12) && ((f12 - 2.0*floor(f12 * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f13 = floor(div13);  k_is_13 = (f13 == div13) && ((f13 - 2.0*floor(f13 * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f14 = floor(div14);  k_is_14 = (f14 == div14) && ((f14 - 2.0*floor(f14 * 0.5)) >= 0.5);
f15 = floor(div15);  k_is_15 = (f15 == div15) && ((f15 - 2.0*floor(f15 * 0.5)) >= 0.5);

// 1/sqrt(16) for unit-RMS normalization of the 16-bin sum.
PINK_NORM = 0.25;


// --- Pink generator macro for L channel ---


// Sixteen independent noise() draws (decorrelated by call site).
nL_0  = noise();  nL_1  = noise();  nL_2  = noise();  nL_3  = noise();
nL_4  = noise();  nL_5  = noise();  nL_6  = noise();  nL_7  = noise();
nL_8  = noise();  nL_9  = noise();  nL_10 = noise();  nL_11 = noise();
nL_12 = noise();  nL_13 = noise();  nL_14 = noise();  nL_15 = noise();

// Conditionally update each bin. gen~ noise() outputs uniform [-1, 1];
// we want roughly N(0, 1)-style variance to match the numpy ref. The
// standard deviation of U(-1, 1) is 1/sqrt(3) ~= 0.577. We therefore
// scale each draw by sqrt(3) ~= 1.7320508 so the bin variance matches
// N(0, 1). After summing 16 bins and dividing by sqrt(16)=4, output
// variance is 1.0 (matching numpy ref's normalization).
SQRT3 = 1.7320508075688772;

pBinL_0  = (k_is_0  != 0) ? (nL_0  * SQRT3) : pBinL_0;
pBinL_1  = (k_is_1  != 0) ? (nL_1  * SQRT3) : pBinL_1;
pBinL_2  = (k_is_2  != 0) ? (nL_2  * SQRT3) : pBinL_2;
pBinL_3  = (k_is_3  != 0) ? (nL_3  * SQRT3) : pBinL_3;
pBinL_4  = (k_is_4  != 0) ? (nL_4  * SQRT3) : pBinL_4;
pBinL_5  = (k_is_5  != 0) ? (nL_5  * SQRT3) : pBinL_5;
pBinL_6  = (k_is_6  != 0) ? (nL_6  * SQRT3) : pBinL_6;
pBinL_7  = (k_is_7  != 0) ? (nL_7  * SQRT3) : pBinL_7;
pBinL_8  = (k_is_8  != 0) ? (nL_8  * SQRT3) : pBinL_8;
pBinL_9  = (k_is_9  != 0) ? (nL_9  * SQRT3) : pBinL_9;
pBinL_10 = (k_is_10 != 0) ? (nL_10 * SQRT3) : pBinL_10;
pBinL_11 = (k_is_11 != 0) ? (nL_11 * SQRT3) : pBinL_11;
pBinL_12 = (k_is_12 != 0) ? (nL_12 * SQRT3) : pBinL_12;
pBinL_13 = (k_is_13 != 0) ? (nL_13 * SQRT3) : pBinL_13;
pBinL_14 = (k_is_14 != 0) ? (nL_14 * SQRT3) : pBinL_14;
pBinL_15 = (k_is_15 != 0) ? (nL_15 * SQRT3) : pBinL_15;

pinkL = (pBinL_0  + pBinL_1  + pBinL_2  + pBinL_3
       + pBinL_4  + pBinL_5  + pBinL_6  + pBinL_7
       + pBinL_8  + pBinL_9  + pBinL_10 + pBinL_11
       + pBinL_12 + pBinL_13 + pBinL_14 + pBinL_15) * PINK_NORM;


// --- Pink generator macro for R channel (independent noise() streams) ---


nR_0  = noise();  nR_1  = noise();  nR_2  = noise();  nR_3  = noise();
nR_4  = noise();  nR_5  = noise();  nR_6  = noise();  nR_7  = noise();
nR_8  = noise();  nR_9  = noise();  nR_10 = noise();  nR_11 = noise();
nR_12 = noise();  nR_13 = noise();  nR_14 = noise();  nR_15 = noise();

pBinR_0  = (k_is_0  != 0) ? (nR_0  * SQRT3) : pBinR_0;
pBinR_1  = (k_is_1  != 0) ? (nR_1  * SQRT3) : pBinR_1;
pBinR_2  = (k_is_2  != 0) ? (nR_2  * SQRT3) : pBinR_2;
pBinR_3  = (k_is_3  != 0) ? (nR_3  * SQRT3) : pBinR_3;
pBinR_4  = (k_is_4  != 0) ? (nR_4  * SQRT3) : pBinR_4;
pBinR_5  = (k_is_5  != 0) ? (nR_5  * SQRT3) : pBinR_5;
pBinR_6  = (k_is_6  != 0) ? (nR_6  * SQRT3) : pBinR_6;
pBinR_7  = (k_is_7  != 0) ? (nR_7  * SQRT3) : pBinR_7;
pBinR_8  = (k_is_8  != 0) ? (nR_8  * SQRT3) : pBinR_8;
pBinR_9  = (k_is_9  != 0) ? (nR_9  * SQRT3) : pBinR_9;
pBinR_10 = (k_is_10 != 0) ? (nR_10 * SQRT3) : pBinR_10;
pBinR_11 = (k_is_11 != 0) ? (nR_11 * SQRT3) : pBinR_11;
pBinR_12 = (k_is_12 != 0) ? (nR_12 * SQRT3) : pBinR_12;
pBinR_13 = (k_is_13 != 0) ? (nR_13 * SQRT3) : pBinR_13;
pBinR_14 = (k_is_14 != 0) ? (nR_14 * SQRT3) : pBinR_14;
pBinR_15 = (k_is_15 != 0) ? (nR_15 * SQRT3) : pBinR_15;

pinkR = (pBinR_0  + pBinR_1  + pBinR_2  + pBinR_3
       + pBinR_4  + pBinR_5  + pBinR_6  + pBinR_7
       + pBinR_8  + pBinR_9  + pBinR_10 + pBinR_11
       + pBinR_12 + pBinR_13 + pBinR_14 + pBinR_15) * PINK_NORM;


// =====================================================================
// HISS RBJ BIQUAD COEFFICIENTS (HPF 50 Hz / LPF 12 kHz, Butterworth)
// =====================================================================
//
// RBJ Audio EQ Cookbook formulas, recomputed each sample from
// `samplerate` so the filters track SR changes (no [js] companion
// needed for these). Cost: ~12 trig calls per sample, all on constants
// -> the gen~ compiler will hoist them as long as samplerate is held
// stable; on SR transitions there's a momentary bump (~5 ms) which is
// inaudible against the noise floor.

// --- HPF 50 Hz Q=0.7071 ---
hpf_w0     = TWO_PI * HISS_HPF_HZ / samplerate;
hpf_cosw0  = cos(hpf_w0);
hpf_alpha  = sin(hpf_w0) / (2.0 * HISS_HPF_Q);
hpf_a0     = 1.0 + hpf_alpha;
hpf_b0     = ((1.0 + hpf_cosw0) * 0.5) / hpf_a0;
hpf_b1     = (-(1.0 + hpf_cosw0))      / hpf_a0;
hpf_b2     = ((1.0 + hpf_cosw0) * 0.5) / hpf_a0;
hpf_a1     = (-2.0 * hpf_cosw0)        / hpf_a0;
hpf_a2     = (1.0 - hpf_alpha)         / hpf_a0;

// --- LPF 12 kHz Q=0.7071 ---
lpf_w0     = TWO_PI * HISS_LPF_HZ / samplerate;
lpf_cosw0  = cos(lpf_w0);
lpf_alpha  = sin(lpf_w0) / (2.0 * HISS_LPF_Q);
lpf_a0     = 1.0 + lpf_alpha;
lpf_b0     = ((1.0 - lpf_cosw0) * 0.5) / lpf_a0;
lpf_b1     = (1.0 - lpf_cosw0)         / lpf_a0;
lpf_b2     = ((1.0 - lpf_cosw0) * 0.5) / lpf_a0;
lpf_a1     = (-2.0 * lpf_cosw0)        / lpf_a0;
lpf_a2     = (1.0 - lpf_alpha)         / lpf_a0;

// --- VCR LPF 200 Hz Q=0.9 ---
vcr_w0     = TWO_PI * VCR_LPF_HZ / samplerate;
vcr_cosw0  = cos(vcr_w0);
vcr_alpha  = sin(vcr_w0) / (2.0 * VCR_LPF_Q);
vcr_a0     = 1.0 + vcr_alpha;
vcr_b0     = ((1.0 - vcr_cosw0) * 0.5) / vcr_a0;
vcr_b1     = (1.0 - vcr_cosw0)         / vcr_a0;
vcr_b2     = ((1.0 - vcr_cosw0) * 0.5) / vcr_a0;
vcr_a1     = (-2.0 * vcr_cosw0)        / vcr_a0;
vcr_a2     = (1.0 - vcr_alpha)         / vcr_a0;


// =====================================================================
// HISS PATH -- HPF 50 then LPF 12k, per channel (Direct Form I)
// =====================================================================

// --- HPF Channel L ---

hpfL_y = hpf_b0*pinkL + hpf_b1*hpfL_x1 + hpf_b2*hpfL_x2
       - hpf_a1*hpfL_y1 - hpf_a2*hpfL_y2;
hpfL_y = (abs(hpfL_y) < 1e-30) ? 0.0 : hpfL_y;       // denormal flush

hpfL_x2 = hpfL_x1;
hpfL_x1 = pinkL;
hpfL_y2 = hpfL_y1;
hpfL_y1 = hpfL_y;

// --- LPF Channel L ---

lpfL_y = lpf_b0*hpfL_y + lpf_b1*lpfL_x1 + lpf_b2*lpfL_x2
       - lpf_a1*lpfL_y1 - lpf_a2*lpfL_y2;
lpfL_y = (abs(lpfL_y) < 1e-30) ? 0.0 : lpfL_y;

lpfL_x2 = lpfL_x1;
lpfL_x1 = hpfL_y;
lpfL_y2 = lpfL_y1;
lpfL_y1 = lpfL_y;

shapedHissL = lpfL_y;

// --- HPF Channel R ---

hpfR_y = hpf_b0*pinkR + hpf_b1*hpfR_x1 + hpf_b2*hpfR_x2
       - hpf_a1*hpfR_y1 - hpf_a2*hpfR_y2;
hpfR_y = (abs(hpfR_y) < 1e-30) ? 0.0 : hpfR_y;

hpfR_x2 = hpfR_x1;
hpfR_x1 = pinkR;
hpfR_y2 = hpfR_y1;
hpfR_y1 = hpfR_y;

// --- LPF Channel R ---

lpfR_y = lpf_b0*hpfR_y + lpf_b1*lpfR_x1 + lpf_b2*lpfR_x2
       - lpf_a1*lpfR_y1 - lpf_a2*lpfR_y2;
lpfR_y = (abs(lpfR_y) < 1e-30) ? 0.0 : lpfR_y;

lpfR_x2 = lpfR_x1;
lpfR_x1 = hpfR_y;
lpfR_y2 = lpfR_y1;
lpfR_y1 = lpfR_y;

shapedHissR = lpfR_y;

// Hiss output (gated by hiss_on, scaled by quadratic-tapered hiss_gain).
hissL = (hiss_on != 0) ? (shapedHissL * hiss_gain) : 0.0;
hissR = (hiss_on != 0) ? (shapedHissR * hiss_gain) : 0.0;


// =====================================================================
// MECHANICAL PATH -- VCR sub-engine (decorrelated stereo)
// =====================================================================
//
// White noise -> LPF 200 Hz Q=0.9 -> slow-AM multiplier.

// --- White noise per channel (independent streams via separate noise() sites) ---
whiteVcrL = noise() * SQRT3;   // U(-1,1) -> match unit-variance scale
whiteVcrR = noise() * SQRT3;

// --- VCR LPF Channel L (Direct Form I) ---

vcrLpfL = vcr_b0*whiteVcrL + vcr_b1*vcrL_x1 + vcr_b2*vcrL_x2
        - vcr_a1*vcrL_y1   - vcr_a2*vcrL_y2;
vcrLpfL = (abs(vcrLpfL) < 1e-30) ? 0.0 : vcrLpfL;

vcrL_x2 = vcrL_x1;
vcrL_x1 = whiteVcrL;
vcrL_y2 = vcrL_y1;
vcrL_y1 = vcrLpfL;

// --- VCR LPF Channel R ---

vcrLpfR = vcr_b0*whiteVcrR + vcr_b1*vcrR_x1 + vcr_b2*vcrR_x2
        - vcr_a1*vcrR_y1   - vcr_a2*vcrR_y2;
vcrLpfR = (abs(vcrLpfR) < 1e-30) ? 0.0 : vcrLpfR;

vcrR_x2 = vcrR_x1;
vcrR_x1 = whiteVcrR;
vcrR_y2 = vcrR_y1;
vcrR_y1 = vcrLpfR;

// Normalize to ~ unit peak (numpy ref does per-buffer; we use a fixed
// gain). See VCR_GAIN_FIX comment in constants block.
vcrL = vcrLpfL * VCR_GAIN_FIX;
vcrR = vcrLpfR * VCR_GAIN_FIX;

// --- Slow-AM modulator per channel (cascaded 1-pole at 0.6 Hz) ---
// Coefficient: a = 1 - exp(-2*pi*fc/sr).
am_a = 1.0 - exp(-TWO_PI * VCR_AM_LFO_HZ / samplerate);

nAmL = noise();                               // independent stream
amL_a = amL_a + am_a * (nAmL  - amL_a);
amL_b = amL_b + am_a * (amL_a - amL_b);       // 12 dB/oct cascade
walkL = clamp(amL_b, -1.0, 1.0);
amMultL = VCR_AM_CENTER + (VCR_AM_DEPTH * 0.5) * walkL;

nAmR = noise();
amR_a = amR_a + am_a * (nAmR  - amR_a);
amR_b = amR_b + am_a * (amR_a - amR_b);
walkR = clamp(amR_b, -1.0, 1.0);
amMultR = VCR_AM_CENTER + (VCR_AM_DEPTH * 0.5) * walkR;

vcrOutL = vcrL * amMultL;
vcrOutR = vcrR * amMultR;


// =====================================================================
// MECHANICAL PATH -- HUM sub-engine (mono / correlated)
// =====================================================================
//
// Sine bank: h=1,2,3,4,5,7 of the fundamental, with relative amps
// [1.00, 0.30, 0.45, 0.18, 0.22, 0.12]. Phase accumulators wrap at
// 2*pi via subtraction. Output is identical for L and R (mono pickup).

// Phase increments per harmonic (rad/sample). samplerate-aware.
phinc1 = TWO_PI * (HUM_FUNDAMENTAL_HZ * 1.0) / samplerate;
phinc2 = TWO_PI * (HUM_FUNDAMENTAL_HZ * 2.0) / samplerate;
phinc3 = TWO_PI * (HUM_FUNDAMENTAL_HZ * 3.0) / samplerate;
phinc4 = TWO_PI * (HUM_FUNDAMENTAL_HZ * 4.0) / samplerate;
phinc5 = TWO_PI * (HUM_FUNDAMENTAL_HZ * 5.0) / samplerate;
phinc7 = TWO_PI * (HUM_FUNDAMENTAL_HZ * 7.0) / samplerate;


// Advance + wrap to [-pi, pi].
nph1 = humPh1 + phinc1;
nph1 = (nph1 >  PI) ? (nph1 - TWO_PI) : nph1;
nph1 = (nph1 < -PI) ? (nph1 + TWO_PI) : nph1;
humPh1 = nph1;

nph2 = humPh2 + phinc2;
nph2 = (nph2 >  PI) ? (nph2 - TWO_PI) : nph2;
nph2 = (nph2 < -PI) ? (nph2 + TWO_PI) : nph2;
humPh2 = nph2;

nph3 = humPh3 + phinc3;
nph3 = (nph3 >  PI) ? (nph3 - TWO_PI) : nph3;
nph3 = (nph3 < -PI) ? (nph3 + TWO_PI) : nph3;
humPh3 = nph3;

nph4 = humPh4 + phinc4;
nph4 = (nph4 >  PI) ? (nph4 - TWO_PI) : nph4;
nph4 = (nph4 < -PI) ? (nph4 + TWO_PI) : nph4;
humPh4 = nph4;

nph5 = humPh5 + phinc5;
nph5 = (nph5 >  PI) ? (nph5 - TWO_PI) : nph5;
nph5 = (nph5 < -PI) ? (nph5 + TWO_PI) : nph5;
humPh5 = nph5;

nph7 = humPh7 + phinc7;
nph7 = (nph7 >  PI) ? (nph7 - TWO_PI) : nph7;
nph7 = (nph7 < -PI) ? (nph7 + TWO_PI) : nph7;
humPh7 = nph7;

humSum = 1.00 * sin(nph1)
       + 0.30 * sin(nph2)
       + 0.45 * sin(nph3)
       + 0.18 * sin(nph4)
       + 0.22 * sin(nph5)
       + 0.12 * sin(nph7);

hum = humSum * HUM_NORMALIZE;   // mono signal


// =====================================================================
// MECHANICAL CROSSFADE (linear, hard silent center) + GATE
// =====================================================================
//
// vcr * vcr_w  +  hum * hum_w, all scaled by common_gain. The hum path
// is added IDENTICALLY to L and R (mono-correlated); halve the per-
// channel contribution so the stereo-summed peak still hits the
// MECH_TARGET_PEAK_AMP target (matches numpy ref's hum_scaled * 0.5).

humContrib   = hum * hum_w * common_gain * 0.5;       // halve for mono dup
mechContribL = (vcrOutL * vcr_w * common_gain) + humContrib;
mechContribR = (vcrOutR * vcr_w * common_gain) + humContrib;

mechL = (mech_on != 0) ? mechContribL : 0.0;
mechR = (mech_on != 0) ? mechContribR : 0.0;


// =====================================================================
// FINAL SUM (additive on input pass-through)
// =====================================================================
//
// At noise_mode=OFF we short-circuit to the unmodified input to
// guarantee bit-identical pass-through (no rounding from a +0.0 add).

noiseSumL = hissL + mechL;
noiseSumR = hissR + mechR;

out1 = (mode_off != 0) ? in1 : (in1 + noiseSumL);
out2 = (mode_off != 0) ? in2 : (in2 + noiseSumR);

// =====================================================================
// END tl_noise.gendsp
// =====================================================================
